{
  "decline_reason": "",
  "claims": [
    {
      "text": "For an opposition-based provisional refusal concerning an IRDA, the Manual says IP Australia must notify the International Bureau (IB) within four months of publication of acceptance where no extension of time to file the notice of opposition has been granted.",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "ref": "TMM/Part60/4/16/4/16/2",
          "quote": "IP Australia must notify the IB of any opposition within: four months of the publication of acceptance, or seven months of the publication of acceptance where an extension of time for filing Notice of Opposition has been granted."
        }
      ],
      "authority": "manual"
    },
    {
      "text": "Where an extension of time for filing the notice of opposition has been granted, the Manual gives a deadline of seven months from publication of acceptance.",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "ref": "TMM/Part60/4/16/4/16/2",
          "quote": "seven months of the publication of acceptance where an extension of time for filing Notice of Opposition has been granted."
        }
      ],
      "authority": "manual"
    },
    {
      "text": "For notification after the 18-month refusal period, the Manual also requires that the IB have been notified, before that period expired, of the possibility of oppositions being filed after its expiry.",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "ref": "TMM/Part60/4/17/4/17/2~1",
          "quote": "Article 5 of the Madrid Protocol explains that an office may notify a refusal of protection after the expiry of the 18 month time limit in certain circumstances: IF it has notified the IB, before the expiry of the 18-month time limit, of the possibility of oppositions being filed after the expiry of the 18-month time limit"
        }
      ],
      "authority": "manual"
    },
    {
      "text": "Operationally, the Manual directs IP Australia to notify the IB as soon as practicable once the complete notice of opposition is filed, rather than wait until the deadline.",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "ref": "TMM/Part60/4/17/4/17/2~2",
          "quote": "When a complete Notice of Opposition is filed (that is, a notice of intention to oppose, together with an adequate statement of grounds and particulars), IP Australia will record it on the Trade Marks Office database and notify the IB as soon practicable to ensure that time limits are met (Article 5)."
        }
      ],
      "authority": "manual"
    },
    {
      "text": "The legislative obligation supplied here is regulation 17A.32(2), which requires the Registrar to notify the IB of the filing of the notice of opposition in accordance with rule 17, as applicable, of the Madrid Regulations.",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "ref": "TMR1995/r17A.32",
          "quote": "(2) The Registrar must notify the International Bureau of the filing of the notice of opposition in accordance with rule 17, as applicable, of the Madrid Regulations."
        }
      ],
      "authority": "legislation"
    },
    {
      "text": "The supplied legislation does not reproduce rule 17 or Article 5, so I cannot independently verify the Manual’s four- and seven-month periods against those instruments here; this is not a complete account of their requirements.",
      "quotes": [],
      "authority": "none"
    }
  ],
